    "cons": [
      "Does not pick anything up, so you still rake or blow leaves into a pile",
      "Trimmer line wears and needs replacing, and sticks slow it down"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The right tool if you already gather leaves with a rake, blower or sweeper and want to shrink the pile for bags or compost.",
    "description": "A standalone leaf mulcher solves a different problem from a leaf vacuum. It does not pick anything up. You rake, blow or sweep leaves into a pile, then feed them into the hopper, and it shreds them into a bag or bin below. The WG430 is the most popular homeowner version, and for large leaf volumes it is faster than any handheld vacuum. Worx rates it at up to 53 gallons of leaves per minute with an 11:1 reduction, so a pile that would fill many lawn bags ends up in one. Instead of a metal blade, it spins lengths of heavy trimmer line, the way a string trimmer does, which makes it more forgiving if a small stick goes in and less likely to need sharpening. The motor is 13 amps and the cutting diameter is 13 inches. Assembly needs no tools, it folds for storage and it ships with 24 replacement lines and a universal bag holder. The line does wear, especially with dry, gritty leaves, and you need to keep twigs and stones out. Pair it with a sweeper or blower and it is the most efficient setup here for big trees."
  }
