            <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Alex Rivers</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-widest">Home Improvement Editor</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="h-10 w-px bg-gray-200 hidden sm:block"></div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block">
          <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Last Updated</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">{dates.display}</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="12.7k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#how-we-picked" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. How We Picked</a>
          <a href="#types" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. Types of Leaf Vacuum</a>
          <a href="#top-picks" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. Our 6 Top Picks</a>
          <a href="#mulch-ratios" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. What Mulch Ratios Mean</a>
          <a href="#vac-vs-alternatives" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Vacuum vs Blower or Sweeper</a>
          <a href="#corded-vs-cordless" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Corded vs Cordless</a>
          <a href="#wet-leaves" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Wet Leaves and Clogs</a>
          <a href="#using-it" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Getting the Most From One</a>
          <a href="#maintenance" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. Safety and Maintenance</a>
          <a href="#who-should-skip" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">10. Who Should Skip One</a>
          <a href="#mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">11. Common Mistakes</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">12. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          A leaf vacuum is the right tool for beds, patios and small to mid-sized yards, and the wrong one for an acre of oaks. Buy a corded blower-vac with a metal impeller for most yards, go cordless only if you are far from an outlet, and pair a sweeper with a standalone mulcher if you have big trees.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="how-we-picked" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How We Picked
          </h2>

          <p>
            A leaf vacuum has to do three things well: pull leaves off the ground, shred them so the bag holds more, and do both without clogging every few minutes. Blowing is a secondary job, and most of the machines here also do it reasonably well, but nobody buys a leaf vacuum for its blower.
          </p>

          <p>
            We compared the three main kinds of leaf vacuum sold to homeowners, corded blower-vac combos, cordless vacuums and standalone mulchers, and looked for models with long track records and large numbers of owner ratings. For each, we checked the manufacturer's figures for airflow, mulch ratio, impeller material and bag size. We have not run these machines through our own test, and the picks rest on published specifications, owner feedback and how each design fits a particular yard.
          </p>

          <p>
            We weighed mulch ratio and impeller material heavily, because they decide how many bags you fill and how long the machine lasts. A plastic impeller chips and cracks when it meets acorns and small sticks. Every pick here uses metal blades or, in the WG430's case, replaceable trimmer line. We also flagged where suction in vacuum mode differs from blowing airflow, because that is the number that matters for leaves on grass.
          </p>

          <p>
            Ratings are Amazon averages with counts rounded down. Where a model is too new to have many ratings, we say so in its card rather than leave it out, because the cordless category is changing fast and the newest machines are the strongest.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Blower-Vac, Cordless Vac, Mulcher or Lawn Vacuum
          </h2>

          <p>
            Corded blower-vacs are the classic type and still the best value. A 12-amp motor drives an impeller that either pushes air out of a blower tube or pulls it through a vacuum tube and into a shoulder bag. The Toro, Worx WG509 and Black+Decker here are all this type. They have unlimited run time and strong suction, and the cord is the price you pay.
          </p>

          <p>
            Cordless leaf vacuums are catching up. Older battery blower-vacs had little suction, but the newest machines like the EGO LV5005 are built around vacuuming and match corded airflow, at least on high. Run time on full power is the limit, so they suit small and mid-sized yards, or owners with spare batteries.
          </p>

          <p>
